# The Extraordinary Inertia of “Not Clear”

## 1. “Not clear” did not begin as the conclusion of a foot investigation

The most important thing to understand about the words **“Not clear”** is what came before them.

On **10 April 2017**, the patient went to his GP complaining of **abnormal gait and bilateral foot pain**. The GP physically examined the feet and recorded:

- marked mallet toes;
- onychogryphosis;
- calluses and pressure areas;
- difficulty working on his feet.

The problem was sufficiently concrete that Podiatry was planned. :contentReference[oaicite:0]{index=0}

Over the following weeks, however, the GP realised that this was not simply a podiatric presentation.

On **8 June**, he was already considering Neurology specifically for:

> **“gait, feet and ‘muscle tension’ issues”**

and by **29 June** the urgent referral described an unusual stiff/festinant gait, difficulty initiating movement, markedly increased tone, toe-clawing, inability to relax, deteriorating dexterity and functional failure at work.

The GP's question remained deliberately open:

> **“?parkinsonian or other underlying diagnosis.”** :contentReference[oaicite:1]{index=1}

That is the case Neurology received.

The feet were not an incidental symptom added afterwards.

They were part of the problem that caused the neurological referral.

---

## 2. Neurology studied the patient intensively — but the surviving record does not show an equivalent investigation of the feet

The admission itself is extraordinary in its neurological detail.

Neurology examined tone, reflexes, rigidity, bradykinesia, tremor, eye movements, arm swing, posture, coordination, handwriting and gait. The patient was videoed. MRI brain and spine were obtained. CT was performed. Blood tests were undertaken. Young-onset Parkinsonism was identified. Genetics were considered. The case was discussed as complex.

The feet do appear in that assessment.

The neurologist records:

> **pes planus**

and:

> **hammertoes**

and describes the gait as:

> **“almost with a footdrop appearance somewhat suggestive of a dystonic posture - NOT FOOT DROP.”** :contentReference[oaicite:2]{index=2}

But that is the point.

The surviving documentation contains extensive serial neurological examination and reasoning.

It does **not** contain a comparable serial examination of the painful feet.

There is no documented progression through repeated inspection of the painful sites, pressure points, footwear, loading, callus distribution, reproducibility of the pain, relation of toe posture to pain, effect of altered posture on the painful areas, or repeated examination of the feet under different neurological states.

The record documents that the feet existed.

It documents some structural findings.

It documents the patient's history of longstanding Podiatry.

But there is no surviving documentary trail showing that the central reciprocal question was investigated:

> **What are these painful and structurally abnormal feet doing to the gait, and what is the neurological disorder doing to the feet?**

The distinction matters enormously.

**“Not clear” sounds like the conclusion of an inquiry.**

On the face of the surviving record, it is at least equally capable of being the consequence of an inquiry that was never carried through.

---

## 3. The feet were effectively routed out while the neurological case accelerated

By **6 July**, while the patient was still in hospital, the GP had contacted Podiatry and recorded that the case would be prioritised:

> **“as Parkinson’s.”** :contentReference[oaicite:3]{index=3}

The short discharge record subsequently says:

> **“Podiatry referral has been sent to review issues with feet reported by the patient.”** :contentReference[oaicite:4]{index=4}

That wording is revealing.

The neurological syndrome is investigated in depth.

The feet become **“issues with feet reported by the patient”** for Podiatry.

Podiatry was not unreasonable.

There were real corns, calluses, deformity and pressure lesions.

The patient had also had Podiatry before. On his evidence, he therefore already knew what Podiatry could and could not do for him.

What is not documented is an inpatient discussion asking whether returning him to the same kind of structural treatment actually answered the neurological question raised by the feet.

There is no documented inquiry:

> **You have already had years of Podiatry. What has it fixed? What has it not fixed? Why are the feet now appearing inside this extraordinary neurological presentation?**

Instead, the structural pathway continues.

And the neurological pathway continues.

That is the origin of the partition.

---

## 4. “Not clear” was therefore a statement about an unresolved relationship — not proof that the relationship had been tested

The long discharge account ultimately says that the leg/foot symptoms, including corns, calluses and burning:

> **“may have been completely separate”**

or perhaps represented:

> **“a secondary peripheral neuropathy”**

and then concludes:

> **“Not clear.”** :contentReference[oaicite:5]{index=5}

Read casually, that sounds appropriately cautious.

Read against the actual sequence, it means something more consequential.

Neurology had established Parkinsonism.

It had identified a dystonic-looking lower-limb phenomenon.

It had observed structural feet.

It had gathered a history of longstanding painful feet.

But the surviving record does not show that the interaction between those things was actually tested.

So the original **“Not clear”** is not:

> **We investigated the relationship thoroughly and the answer remains genuinely indeterminate.**

The documentary position is closer to:

> **We identified both sides of the problem but did not resolve how they interact.**

That is a very different kind of uncertainty.

And it matters because unresolved uncertainty requires a future route back into inquiry.

The discharge account contains no obvious exit condition for “Not clear.”

There is no:

> if neurological treatment changes the feet, reconsider this;

no:

> if toe posture changes with medication, reconsider this;

no:

> if Podiatry repeatedly treats the same lesion without resolving the pain, reconsider this;

no:

> if levodopa later modifies the feet, reconstruct the original relationship.

The uncertainty is recorded.

But no mechanism is recorded for ending it.

---

## 5. Then the patient himself forces the missing question back into the room

On the patient's direct evidence, before discharge he realised the simplest thing in the world.

He had originally gone to a doctor because his feet hurt and he could not walk properly.

He had now spent roughly a week undergoing an extraordinary neurological admission.

He had been diagnosed with Parkinsonism.

Yet, from his perspective, the feet themselves had scarcely been examined.

So he protested.

His account is detailed.

Four medical students were present.

One was asked to fetch Dr Thomas Monaghan.

The patient used the NCT / bald-tyres analogy:

> **“So are you telling me you’re going to bring me in here, give me the full NCT, and send me out on bald tires?”** :contentReference[oaicite:6]{index=6}

The meaning is elementary.

He was not disputing Parkinsonism.

He was not demanding levodopa.

He was not offering a neurological theory.

He was saying:

> **You have examined everything except the part that brought me here.**

That Protest is important because it transforms the status of the unresolved feet.

Before the Protest, Neurology might simply have regarded Podiatry as a reasonable continuation.

After the Protest, the treating neurologist is confronted with the patient's explicit claim that the proposed disposition does not answer the presenting problem.

A choice now exists.

The neurologist can continue with the existing plan.

Or he can go back.

He can re-read the referral.

He can reconsider the feet.

He can ask why a patient referred with gait, feet, toe-clawing and pathological tension has received an extensive movement-disorders assessment without an equivalent investigation of the painful weight-bearing structures through which the gait is expressed.

He can reconsider whether Podiatry is enough.

He can take the question back into the senior neurological discussion.

That is the fork.

---

## 6. The teaching setting makes the fork more consequential, not less

On the patient's account, this did not occur privately in an incidental corridor conversation.

It occurred in a **bedside teaching interaction involving four medical trainees**.

That means the event occupied two environments simultaneously.

It was a clinical encounter.

It was also an educational encounter.

The patient was effectively being used as a teaching case while challenging the adequacy of the case formulation in front of the trainees.

That is an unusually serious moment.

The patient being studied is telling the clinicians and students:

> **the case you are studying is missing the thing I came in with.**

The later Article 15 request sought clinical notes, student records, teaching records, ward-round records, trainee assignments and governance material capable of testing that recollection. The later hospital response said that several potentially relevant categories, including historical ward-round and trainee-related material, were not held. The surviving consultant account therefore has much greater documentary durability than the teaching interaction the patient remembers. :contentReference[oaicite:7]{index=7}

Whatever the eventual evidential finding about that interaction, its clinical meaning is straightforward.

The Protest placed the omitted question directly before the treating neurologist.

---

## 7. Then “Not clear” enters the definitive management account

The extraordinary feature of the discharge document is that it was not already frozen at the moment of physical discharge.

The document describes itself as being partly for the patient and intended:

> **“to consolidate a lot of discussions which we have had”**

to help with:

> **“debriefing”**

and:

> **“to set out for him a clear plan.”**

It later incorporates the preceding narrative into the formal plan through:

> **“As per above.”** :contentReference[oaicite:8]{index=8}

The completed artefact also remained open after physical discharge.

It absorbed the later DaTscan.

It contains a transcription-finalisation addendum.

It was finally consultant-confirmed later.

Yet the Protest does not appear.

The final document instead carries forward:

**Parkinsonism**

plus:

**deliberate levodopa restraint**

plus:

**Podiatry**

plus:

**possible separate/neuropathic feet**

plus:

> **“Not clear.”**

That is the moment at which “Not clear” changes character.

It is no longer merely the neurologist saying:

> **I do not yet know.**

It becomes part of the management plan transmitted forward.

