# The beautiful first year

And then something remarkable happens.

Medicine begins to answer the question.

The patient is discharged on rasagiline and procyclidine.

The response is extraordinary.

His gait comes back together.

His writing improves.

His posture improves.

His body becomes loose and limber.

He runs.

He jumps.

He hops.

He skips.

He describes himself as:

> **“living in a miracle, literally.”**

This is the beautiful part of the case.

The GP had asked an intelligent open question.

Neurology had identified a real disease.

A cautious treatment strategy had been chosen.

Treatment worked spectacularly.

And then nature supplied an experiment.

During withdrawal of procyclidine, the patient wrote:

> **“my feet are restless and my walk is falling apart.”**

By April 2018, that information had entered the neurologist's own record.

When procyclidine was stopped, the **first thing** to change rapidly was the phenomenon at the outside of the left foot.

The patient became more “shifty” and “unrelaxed.”

The same neurologist wrote that levodopa or a dopamine agonist could make him:

> **“very much better”**

while continuing the policy of deferring those treatments. :contentReference[oaicite:9]{index=9}

This should have made the unresolved question easier.

The important observation was no longer simply:

> **the patient has structural foot disease.**

It was now:

> **the state of the foot problem changes rapidly when neurological medication changes.**

That is exactly the sort of information that could clarify the July **“Not clear.”**

---

## 8. But the calibration point did not produce a documented reconstruction of the original question

This is where the inertia begins.

The April 2018 observation did not require anyone to conclude:

> the corn itself is neurological.

That would be absurd.

A structural lesion does not vanish and reappear over a short medication interval.

The important inference is much more modest:

> **the patient's experience at that foot site was dynamically modifiable by neurological state.**

That fact required the structural and neurological stories to be reconsidered together.

Instead, the record continues to permit them to exist in parallel.

Podiatry continues treating the physical feet.

Neurology continues treating Parkinsonism.

The relationship remains unresolved.

---

## 9. Podiatry then supplies a remarkable anatomical record

The later Podiatry record makes the April 2018 observation much more important.

Over repeated visits, Podiatry documents recurrent structural problems, including repeated pathology around the **left fifth metatarsophalangeal region**, offloading, callus treatment, clawed toes and mechanical management. The 2019 re-referral explicitly links the foot problems to:

> **increased tone and abnormal gait relating to Parkinson's disease**

and describes:

> **“clawing feet” and pain**

while the risk assessment records:

> **“No neuropathy.”** :contentReference[oaicite:10]{index=10}

This does not prove that neuropathy was impossible.

It does something more useful.

It shows that another institutional service was repeatedly observing the structural consequences while explicitly recognising Parkinsonian tone and gait as relevant.

So by this stage the records contain two halves of an experiment:

> **Podiatry knows where the problem repeatedly occurs.**

> **Neurology knows that what happens at that site changes with neurological treatment state.**

Yet the surviving record contains no identifiable moment when those two streams are brought together and the July question is rebuilt.

---

## 10. That is the first real meaning of the inertia of “Not clear”

“Not clear” does not survive because nobody learns anything.

It survives because each service can continue to know its own part.

Podiatry can know callus.

Neurology can know dystonia.

Podiatry can know pressure.

Neurology can know OFF.

Podiatry can know clawed toes.

Neurology can know Parkinsonian foot movement.

Neurology can know medication response.

And still nobody has to answer:

> **What is the relationship between all of these things in this patient?**

That is the inertia.

---

## 11. The later record becomes increasingly neurological without the old uncertainty disappearing

The subsequent record progressively brings individual lower-limb phenomena inside the neurological disorder.

A foot throw is later described as:

> **“actually parkinsonian.”**

Cramping raises Parkinsonian dystonia.

OFF dystonia is considered.

A short-stepped gait is understood through OFF Parkinsonism.

Stalevo affects function and pain.

Sinemet affects the feet.

These are genuine advances.

They are not evidence that Neurology never understood anything about the feet.

They demonstrate something more extraordinary.

> **The record becomes increasingly knowledgeable while the original causal partition remains intact enough to survive the new knowledge.**

A particular movement can be Parkinsonian.

A particular cramp can be dystonic.

A particular shuffle can be OFF.

But the underlying chronic painful feet can still remain something else.

---

## 12. By 2023 the old uncertainty has acquired a pharmacological consequence

By this point the patient has also adopted the clinical vocabulary that has been supplied to him.

Neuropathy becomes part of the language in which he describes his pain.

That matters because repetition of a medical label by the patient is not automatically independent confirmation of the label.

A hypothesis can travel like this:

> **Neurology suggests neuropathy → patient learns “neuropathy” → patient tells GP he has neuropathic pain → patient tells Parkinson's nurse he has neuropathic pain → “neuropathic pain” returns to Neurology inside the patient's reported history.**

The provenance of the word can disappear.

The phenomenon itself remains.

---

## 13. March 2023 shows the consequences

The GP sends an urgent referral describing severe bilateral foot and lower-leg pain.

The patient is taking **600 mg/day of pregabalin/Lyrica**.

The pain is extreme.

The referral says it is:

> **“causing death wish”**

and the GP writes:

> **“I am worried about him.”**

The Movement Disorders ANP then records that, **despite the Lyrica**, the patient has debilitating pain and:

> **“no quality of life.”**

This is not evidence that the neuropathic-pain treatment is working.

It is evidence that the patient is at the highest end of that treatment strategy while remaining in severe distress.

And it occurs after the service already possesses the 2018 observation that a foot phenomenon was neurologically modifiable.

---

## 14. Then David Folan briefly breaks through the inherited label

Later in 2023 the patient again describes:

> **“severe neuropathic pain.”**

Folan does not simply accept the label.

He looks at what is happening.

The patient is shuffling.

Mobility is reduced.

It is worse in the morning.

It worsens later when Sinemet has not been taken.

And Folan concludes:

> **“It sounds like his Parkinson’s is being undertreated and the leg pain and shuffling is off related.”**

He proposes additional Parkinson medication.

Dr Monaghan agrees that this is reasonable.

This is perhaps the cleanest demonstration of the whole case.

The communication begins with the inherited category:

> **neuropathic pain**

and ends with the Movement Disorders clinician saying:

> **OFF-related pain and shuffling.**

Locally, the phenomenon has become considerably clearer.

Globally, the old category survives.

