# How “Not clear” became durable

By this stage, **“Not clear” no longer means absence of information.**

The service has information.

The feet are structurally abnormal.

The gait is neurologically abnormal.

The lower-limb posture can be dystonic.

The foot throw can be Parkinsonian.

The cramps can be dystonic.

The pain and shuffling can be OFF-related.

Neurological medication can change the feet.

Maximum-dose pregabalin can fail to control the pain.

And later levodopa can produce dramatic relief.

But the structure of the original uncertainty allows all of those things to coexist without requiring the central relationship to be rebuilt.

That is why “Not clear” is so powerful.

It is not a diagnosis.

It is not even simply uncertainty.

It becomes an **organising permission**.

---

## 15. Pregabalin eventually exposes the two halves of the original management frame acting together

The 2025 complaint response makes this explicit.

Dr Monaghan says that Lyrica was being used for neuropathic pain:

> **“(of whatever cause) in part to try to spare further Sinemet increase”**

and accepts that it had:

> **“incomplete effect at maximum dose.”**

That sentence joins the two parts of the July 2017 management plan.

The original plan had said:

**avoid levodopa as long as reasonably possible**

and:

**the feet may be separate / neuropathic / “Not clear.”**

Years later the operational consequence is:

**Sinemet escalation remains constrained**

while:

**pregabalin is used for the pain partly in order to preserve that restraint.**

The two parts of the frame have become pharmacologically connected.

---

## 16. And then 2025 produces the final experiment

By March 2025 the pattern is almost impossible to miss.

The first steps in the morning are torture.

The feet improve once Parkinson medication reaches the system.

Controlled-release Sinemet is used to extend overnight coverage.

The neurologist later describes the result as:

> **“profound relief.”**

He also says:

> **“we have had a clear understanding for quite some time that Sinemet does help his pain.”**

Yet the same response still contains:

> **“If there is a degree of peripheral neuropathy here…”**

and explains the effect of Sinemet as improving pain:

> **“of whatever cause.”**

That is the mature form of July 2017's **“Not clear.”**

The neurological relationship is now admitted.

The causal category remains open.

---

# The extraordinary argument

The strongest version of the “Not clear” argument is therefore not:

> **Neurology examined the feet thoroughly in 2017, could not determine the answer, and remained uncertain for eight years.**

The documentary chronology does not support that simple story.

The stronger reconstruction is:

> **The feet were physically examined and identified as abnormal before referral. They were part of the neurological referral. During the admission the patient underwent extraordinarily detailed neurological investigation, but the surviving record contains no comparable serial investigation of the painful feet or explicit examination of the interaction between foot pathology and neurological gait. The feet were routed to Podiatry, and the unresolved relationship was recorded as “Not clear.” Before closure, the patient directly challenged that disposition and forced the missing question back before the neurologist. The later-finalised discharge account nevertheless preserved the existing management pathway and the words “Not clear,” while omitting the patient's challenge. Within months, neurological treatment and withdrawal began answering the unresolved question through the patient's body. By April 2018 the treating neurologist himself had recorded rapid neurological modifiability of the outside-left-foot phenomenon. Over the following years Podiatry mapped persistent structural pathology, Neurology progressively identified Parkinsonian, dystonic and OFF-related foot phenomena, maximum-dose pregabalin failed to provide adequate control, and levodopa repeatedly modified the pain and feet. Yet the original unresolved category remained sufficiently durable that in 2025, after profound Sinemet CR relief, peripheral neuropathy could still remain an “if.”**

That is the inertia.

And it starts much earlier than 2025.

It starts with a deceptively simple transformation:

> **“Not clear” was written as though it described the result of uncertainty.**

But the record raises a more uncomfortable possibility:

> **it described the consequence of not having fully investigated the relationship in the first place.**

Then the patient noticed.

He protested.

The plan continued.

And for the next eight years, the record repeatedly accumulated pieces of the answer without requiring the original question to be asked again in full.

# In one sentence

> **The extraordinary history of “Not clear” is that a relationship which had not been fully investigated in July 2017 was converted into a durable management uncertainty, expressly challenged by the patient before discharge, repeatedly illuminated by later treatment and clinical observation, and still capable of remaining unresolved after profound dopaminergic relief eight years later.**
